# DTF tier manifest: scan-grading-os-and-jar-trivy
#   (artifact-keeper#6233, fixed for 1.12.3; the Trivy half of #5262)
# Profile-set: filesystem + a run-time-filled upstream (upstreams.mock-files,
# the same mock as scan-grading-os-and-jar, its control API on the slot's
# TRIVY_PORT). The oracle then starts the scanner adapter (scanners.trivy,
# SCANNER_ADAPTER_IMAGE) and recreates the backend with TRIVY_ADAPTER_URL,
# through fixtures/trivy-adapter.yml, which drops the adapter's host port so
# it does not collide with the mock's. Every scan-on-proxy then runs the
# bundled Grype AND the Trivy filesystem scan through the adapter.
# fixtures/build_fixtures.py builds six Maven archives with fixed timestamps.
# NETWORK: the adapter downloads the Trivy DB from ghcr.io once (~100 MiB,
# pre-warmed in setup, cached in the slot's trivy volume).
#
# THE BUG
# #6233  #5262 (fixed in #5340) kept a JAR's embedded build POM
#        (META-INF/maven/<g>/<a>/pom.xml) out of the Grype directory scan.
#        The Trivy filesystem scan of the same staged workspace still read
#        it, so Trivy graded every dependency the POM DECLARES as if the JAR
#        shipped it, and a fail_closed Remote blocked the JAR on Trivy's
#        findings alone. Since artifact-keeper#6236 the backend drops an
#        embedded build POM whose pom.properties sits beside it before Trivy
#        runs, unless the archive bundles nested libraries (a .war/.ear, a
#        WEB-INF/lib or BOOT-INF/lib directory, or a nested .jar/.war/.ear/
#        .aar/.hpi/.jpi anywhere in the tree), whose build POM may be the
#        only thing naming them. TRIVY_IGNORE_EMBEDDED_POM_DECLARATIONS=false
#        turns the drop off.
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      RED on a backend without #6236, GREEN with it:
#              the JAR whose build POM declares compile-scope log4j-core
#              2.14.1 and jackson-databind 2.9.8, bundling neither: 200
#              X-AK-Scan: clean, verdict clean.
#   (CONTROL)  hold on BOTH images: log4j-core 2.14.1 itself is 403
#              scan_blocked; a WAR whose only record of a bundled log4j-core
#              is its build POM (the WEB-INF/lib JAR has no pom.properties,
#              manifest or version) is 403 scan_blocked; a JAR with a build
#              POM declaring log4j-core and no pom.properties is 403
#              scan_blocked; so are a Spring Boot .jar and an OSGi bundle
#              whose only record of a bundled log4j-core is their build POM
#              (the nested JAR, under BOOT-INF/lib or at the bundle root, has
#              no pom.properties). Grype excludes build POMs, so the last four
#              are Trivy's findings: they show Trivy is in the loop and that
#              the fix keeps the POMs it must.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: requests back to back.
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the mock upstream is on a private subnet.
COVERS="6233"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-files"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
NEEDS_INTERNET="true"
